44.5 Integral Bounds & Variational Representation
The following corollary provides bounds on integrals of bounded measurable functions with respect to probability measures in terms of the total variation distance.

Corollary 44.2 (Bounded Functions Characterization) If \(h : S \to \mathbb{R}\) is a bounded measurable function on \((S, \mathcal{B})\), then for any \(\mu, \nu \in \text{Prob}(S, \mathcal{B})\): \[
\left| \int_S h \, d\mu - \int_S h \, d\nu \right| \le 2 d_{\text{TV}}(\mu, \nu) \cdot \sup_{x \in S} |h(x)|
\] Furthermore, \(d_{\text{TV}}\) has the dual variational formulation: \[
\begin{aligned}
d_{\text{TV}}(\mu, \nu) &= \frac{1}{2} \sup \biggl\{ \left| \int_S h \, d\mu - \int_S h \, d\nu \right| : h : S \to \mathbb{R} \text{ measurable},\\
&\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad \sup_{x \in S} |h(x)| \le 1 \biggr\}
\end{aligned}
\]


Proof (Proof of Corollary 44.2). Fix a dominating measure \(\alpha\) for the pair \(\{\mu, \nu\}\) (e.g. \(\alpha = \frac{1}{2}(\mu + \nu)\)) with \(d\mu = u \, d\alpha\) and \(d\nu = v \, d\alpha\). Since \(h\) is bounded and measurable, \(h \in L^1(\mu) \cap L^1(\nu)\) and all integrals below are finite: \[
\begin{align*}
\left| \int_S h \, d\mu - \int_S h \, d\nu \right| &= \left| \int_S h(u - v) \, d\alpha \right| \le \int_S |h| |u - v| \, d\alpha \\
&\le \left(\sup_{x \in S} |h(x)|\right) \int_S |u - v| \, d\alpha
\end{align*}
\] By Lemma 44.1, \(\int_S |u - v| d\alpha = 2 d_{\text{TV}}(\mu, \nu)\), giving the first bound.
If \(\sup_S |h| \le 1\), this implies \(\left|\int_S h \, d\mu - \int_S h \, d\nu\right| \le 2 d_{\text{TV}}(\mu, \nu)\), so the supremum is at most \(2 d_{\text{TV}}(\mu, \nu)\). To achieve equality, choose \(h(x) = \mathbb{I}_{\{u &gt; v\}}(x) - \mathbb{I}_{\{u \le v\}}(x)\), which is measurable because \(u\) and \(v\) are, and satisfies \(\sup_S |h| = 1 \le 1\). Substituting \(h\): \[
\begin{align*}
\int_S h \, d\mu - \int_S h \, d\nu &= \int_S (\mathbb{I}_{\{u &gt; v\}} - \mathbb{I}_{\{u \le v\}})(u - v) \, d\alpha \\
&= \int_{\{u &gt; v\}} |u - v| \, d\alpha + \int_{\{u \le v\}} |u - v| \, d\alpha \\
&= \int_S |u - v| \, d\alpha = 2 d_{\text{TV}}(\mu, \nu)
\end{align*}
\] Taking the supremum establishes the variational representation. \(\blacksquare\)

52.4 Uniform Distribution
Let \(a&lt;b\) and \(U \sim \operatorname{Unif}([a,b])\). Its density is \(f(x) = \frac{1}{b-a} \mathbf{1}_{[a,b]}(x)\). For \(\xi\ne0\), \[
\begin{align*}
\varphi_U(\xi) &= \int_a^b e^{i\xi x} \frac{1}{b-a} \, dx = \frac{e^{ib\xi} - e^{ia\xi}}{i\xi(b - a)}
\end{align*}
\] At \(\xi=0\), \(\varphi_U(0)=1\), which is also the continuous extension of the displayed formula.






TipSpecial Case: Symmetric Uniform \(\text{Unif}([-b, b])\)



For \(b&gt;0\), setting \(a=-b\) gives the sinc function: \[\varphi_U(\xi) = \frac{e^{ib\xi} - e^{-ib\xi}}{2ib\xi} = \frac{\sin(b\xi)}{b\xi}\] with value \(1\) at \(\xi=0\) by continuous extension.

62.1 Gaussian Integration by Parts & Stein’s Lemma
Recall that if \(X \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)\), then Gaussian integration by parts gives \[
\mathbb{E}[X f(X)] = \mathbb{E}[f'(X)]
\] for every locally absolutely continuous function \(f\) for which the two expectations exist and the Gaussian boundary term vanishes. In particular, the identity holds for bounded absolutely continuous functions with bounded derivative.
Suppose \(X\sim\mathcal N(0,1)\). Then \(X\) has density \(\phi(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-x^2/2}\) and \(\phi'(x)=-x\phi(x)\). Therefore, \(x\phi(x)=-\phi'(x)\). Let \(f\) be absolutely continuous and suppose, for example, that \(f\) and \(f'\) are bounded. Then \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[Xf(X)] &=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}xf(x)\phi(x)\,dx=-\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)\phi'(x)\,dx.
\end{aligned}
\] Applying integration by parts, \[
\begin{aligned}
-\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)\phi'(x)\,dx &=-\left[f(x)\phi(x)\right]_{-\infty}^{\infty} +\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f'(x)\phi(x)\,dx.
\end{aligned}
\] Since \(f\) is bounded and \(\lim_{x\to\pm\infty}\phi(x)=0\), we have \(\lim_{x\to\pm\infty}f(x)\phi(x)=0\). Consequently, the boundary term vanishes, giving \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[Xf(X)] &=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f'(x)\phi(x)\,dx=\mathbb E[f'(X)].
\end{aligned}
\] Hence \[
\boxed{ X\sim\mathcal N(0,1) \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \mathbb E[Xf(X)]=\mathbb E[f'(X)] }
\] for every bounded absolutely continuous \(f\) with bounded derivative. The converse of this identity is also true and forms the foundation of Stein’s method.

36.3 Proof of (4) Product Rule:
Let \(Y \in \mathcal{B}_b(\Omega, \mathcal{G})\) with \(|Y| \le M\). Since \(X \in L^1\) and \(Y\) is bounded, \(XY \in L^1\), so \(\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[XY]\) is well-defined. We prove the claim by verifying that \(Y \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X]\) satisfies the two conditions that uniquely characterize \(\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[XY]\) via Theorem 34.1.

\(\mathcal{G}\)-measurability: Both \(Y\) and \(\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X]\) are \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable, so their product \(Y \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X]\) is \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable. Moreover, \(\|Y \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X]\|_{L^1} \le M \|\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X]\|_{L^1} \le M \|X\|_{L^1} &lt; \infty\), so \(Y \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X] \in L^1(\Omega, \mathcal{G}, P)\).
Averaging property: For any test function \(Z \in \mathcal{B}_b(\Omega, \mathcal{G})\), the product \(YZ\) is also bounded and \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable, i.e. \(YZ \in \mathcal{B}_b(\Omega, \mathcal{G})\). Therefore: \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[(Y \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X]) \cdot Z\right] &= \mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X] \cdot (YZ)\right] && \text{(rearranging)} \\
&= \mathbb{E}\left[X \cdot (YZ)\right] && \text{(averaging property)} \\
&= \mathbb{E}\left[(XY) \cdot Z\right]. && \text{(rearranging)}
\end{align*}
\] Since \(Y \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X]\) is \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable and satisfies \(\mathbb{E}[Y\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X] \cdot Z] = \mathbb{E}[XY \cdot Z]\) for all \(Z \in \mathcal{B}_b(\Omega, \mathcal{G})\), uniqueness in Theorem 34.1 gives \(Y \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[X] = \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{G}}[XY]\) a.s. \(\blacksquare\)

32.1 Proof of (b) Conditional Fatou’s Lemma
Let \(Y_k = \inf_{n \ge k} X_n\). Then \(Y_k \le X_n\) for all \(n \ge k\), and \(Y_k \uparrow \liminf_{n \to \infty} X_n\) as \(k \to \infty\). Monotonicity implies \(\mathbb{E}[Y_k \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}]\) for all \(n \ge k\), so taking the infimum over \(n \ge k\) gives: \[
\mathbb{E}[Y_k \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \inf_{n \ge k} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\] Taking the limit as \(k \to \infty\) and applying the Conditional Monotone Convergence Theorem to \(Y_k \uparrow \liminf_{n \to \infty} X_n\): \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\liminf_{n \to \infty} X_n \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right]& = \lim_{k \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[Y_k \mid \mathcal{G}] \\
&\le \lim_{k \to \infty} \inf_{n \ge k} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}]\\
& = \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{align*}
\]

32.2 Proof of (c) Conditional DCT
First, by the standard Dominated Convergence Theorem, \(X_n \to X\) in \(L^1\), i.e., \(\mathbb{E}[|X_n - X|] \to 0\). By \(L^1\)-contractivity: \[
\begin{align*}
\left\| \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] - \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \right\|_1 &= \left\| \mathbb{E}[X_n - X \mid \mathcal{G}] \right\|_1 \\
&\le \|X_n - X\|_1 \to 0,
\end{align*}
\] which proves \(\mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \to \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}]\) in \(L^1\).
For almost-sure convergence, note that \(0 \le Y \pm X_n \text{ a.s.}\) Apply Conditional Fatou’s Lemma to both sequences:

For \(Y + X_n \ge 0\): \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[Y + X \mid \mathcal{G}] &= \mathbb{E}\left[\liminf_{n \to \infty} (Y + X_n) \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right] \\
&\le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[Y + X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \\
&= \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] + \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{align*}
\] Subtracting \(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] &lt; \infty\) a.s. yields \(\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s.
For \(Y - X_n \ge 0\): \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[Y - X \mid \mathcal{G}] &= \mathbb{E}\left[\liminf_{n \to \infty} (Y - X_n) \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right] \\
&\le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[Y - X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \\
&= \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] - \limsup_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{align*}
\] Subtracting \(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] &lt; \infty\) a.s. and multiplying by \(-1\) yields \(\limsup_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s.

Combining both inequalities yields: \[
\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \limsup_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.,}
\] hence \(\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] = \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s.

75.7 Iterated Conditional Expectations

Theorem 75.2 (Iterated Conditional Expectation Formula) For any \(n \ge k \ge 0\) and any \(g \in \mathcal{B}_b(S, \mathcal{B}_S)\): \[
\mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] = \mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid X_k] = (L^{n-k} g)(X_k) \quad \text{a.s.}
\tag{75.10}\] In particular, setting \(k = 0\): \[
\mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_0] = (L^n g)(X_0) \quad \text{a.s.}
\]


Proof. We proceed by induction on \(m = n - k \ge 0\).

Base Case (\(m = 0\)): For \(n = k\), \(L^0 = I\) (the identity operator). Thus \(\mathbb{E}[g(X_k) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] = g(X_k) = (L^0 g)(X_k)\) a.s.
Induction Step: Assume the statement holds for \(m = n - k - 1 \ge 0\), i.e., \[
\mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_{k+1}] = (L^{n-k-1} g)(X_{k+1}) \quad \text{a.s.}
\] Using the tower property of conditional expectation since \(\mathcal{F}_k \subseteq \mathcal{F}_{k+1}\): \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] &= \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_{k+1}] \mid \mathcal{F}_k] \\
&= \mathbb{E}[(L^{n-k-1} g)(X_{k+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{aligned}
\] Since \(L^{n-k-1} g \in \mathcal{B}_b(S, \mathcal{B}_S)\), we can apply Theorem 75.1 to the bounded measurable function \(\tilde{g} = L^{n-k-1} g\). This gives: \[
\mathbb{E}[\tilde{g}(X_{k+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] = (L \tilde{g})(X_k) = (L (L^{n-k-1} g))(X_k) = (L^{n-k} g)(X_k) \quad \text{a.s.}
\] By mathematical induction, Equation 75.10 holds for all \(n \ge k \ge 0\).

70.2 Integral Convergence Theorems for Conditional Expectation

Theorem 70.1 (Conditional Convergence Theorems) The conditional expectation operator \(\mathbb{E}[\,\cdot \mid \mathcal{G}]\) satisfies the standard integral convergence theorems for non-negative random variables:

Conditional Monotone Convergence Theorem (MCT): If \(0 \le X_n \uparrow X\) a.s., then \[
\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] = \mathbb{E}\left[\lim_{n \to \infty} X_n \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right] = \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\]
Conditional Fatou’s Lemma: If \(X_n \ge 0\) a.s. for all \(n \in \mathbb{N}\), then \[
\mathbb{E}\left[\liminf_{n \to \infty} X_n \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right] \le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\]
Conditional Dominated Convergence Theorem (DCT): If \(X_n \to X\) a.s., \(|X_n| \le Y\) a.s. for all \(n \in \mathbb{N}\), and \(Y \in L^1(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})\), then \[
\mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \to \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s. and in } L^1.
\]



Proof. Proof of (a) Conditional MCT
By the monotonicity of \(\mathbb{E}[\,\cdot \mid \mathcal{G}]\), the sequence \((\mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}])_{n=1}^\infty\) is non-decreasing a.s., so its limit exists a.s. Fix \(B \in \mathcal{G}\). By the standard Monotone Convergence Theorem: \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[ \left(\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}]\right) \mathbb{I}_B \right] &= \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}\big[\mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \mathbb{I}_B\big] \\
&= \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mathbb{I}_B] \\
&= \mathbb{E}\left[\left(\lim_{n \to \infty} X_n\right) \mathbb{I}_B\right] \\
&= \mathbb{E}[X \mathbb{I}_B].
\end{align*}
\] By definition of conditional expectation, this implies \(\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] = \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s.
Proof of (b) Conditional Fatou’s Lemma
Let \(Y_k = \inf_{n \ge k} X_n\). Then \(Y_k \le X_n\) for all \(n \ge k\), and \(Y_k \uparrow \liminf_{n \to \infty} X_n\) as \(k \to \infty\). Monotonicity implies \(\mathbb{E}[Y_k \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}]\) for all \(n \ge k\), so taking the infimum over \(n \ge k\) gives: \[
\mathbb{E}[Y_k \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \inf_{n \ge k} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\] Taking the limit as \(k \to \infty\) and applying the Conditional Monotone Convergence Theorem to \(Y_k \uparrow \liminf_{n \to \infty} X_n\): \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\liminf_{n \to \infty} X_n \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right]& = \lim_{k \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[Y_k \mid \mathcal{G}] \\
&\le \lim_{k \to \infty} \inf_{n \ge k} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}]\\
& = \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{align*}
\]
Proof of (c) Conditional DCT First, by the standard Dominated Convergence Theorem, \(X_n \to X\) in \(L^1\), i.e., \(\mathbb{E}[|X_n - X|] \to 0\). Since \(\pm(X_n - X) \le |X_n - X|\), monotonicity of conditional expectation gives \(|\mathbb{E}[X_n - X \mid \mathcal{G}]| \le \mathbb{E}[|X_n - X| \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s. Taking total expectations: \[
\begin{align*}
\left\| \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] - \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \right\|_1 &= \left\| \mathbb{E}[X_n - X \mid \mathcal{G}] \right\|_1 \\
&\le \mathbb{E}\big[\mathbb{E}[|X_n - X| \mid \mathcal{G}]\big] = \|X_n - X\|_1 \to 0,
\end{align*}
\] which proves \(\mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \to \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}]\) in \(L^1\).
For almost-sure convergence, note that \(0 \le Y \pm X_n \text{ a.s.}\) Apply Conditional Fatou’s Lemma to both sequences:

For \(Y + X_n \ge 0\): \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[Y + X \mid \mathcal{G}] &= \mathbb{E}\left[\liminf_{n \to \infty} (Y + X_n) \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right] \\
&\le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[Y + X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \\
&= \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] + \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{align*}
\] Subtracting \(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] &lt; \infty\) a.s. yields \(\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s.
For \(Y - X_n \ge 0\): \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[Y - X \mid \mathcal{G}] &= \mathbb{E}\left[\liminf_{n \to \infty} (Y - X_n) \;\middle|\; \mathcal{G}\right] \\
&\le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[Y - X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \\
&= \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] - \limsup_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{align*}
\] Subtracting \(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}] &lt; \infty\) a.s. and multiplying by \(-1\) yields \(\limsup_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s.

Combining both inequalities yields: \[
\mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \limsup_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] \le \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] \quad \text{a.s.,}
\] hence \(\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathcal{G}] = \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}]\) a.s.

\(P_K\) is called the orthogonal projection onto \(K\).
If \(K\) has an orthonormal basis \(\{e_n\}\), this projection is given by: \[
P_K(v) = \sum_{n} \langle v, e_n \rangle e_n = \sum_n \left\langle v, \frac{b_n}{\|b_n\|} \right\rangle \frac{b_n}{\|b_n\|}
\] where \(P_K(v) = v\) for \(v \in K\), and \(P_K(w) = 0\) for \(w \in K^\perp\), and \(K^\perp\) is the orthogonal complement of \(K\) in \(H\).
If \(z=v+w\) with \(v \in K\) and \(w \in K^\perp\), then \(P_K(z) = v\): it extracts the \(K\)-component of a vector and discards its \(K^\perp\)-component: \[
\begin{align*}
P_K(z) &= P_K(v + w) = \sum_{n} \langle v + w, e_n \rangle e_n\\
& = \sum_n \langle v, e_n \rangle e_n + \sum_n \langle w, e_n \rangle e_n = v + 0 = v.
\end{align*}
\]

78.2 Discrete-Time Formulation and Induction
When the time index set \(T = \mathbb{N}\) is discrete, the Markov property can be stated in terms of one-step transitions.

Proposition 78.2 (One-Step Characterization in Discrete Time) Let \(T = \mathbb{N}\). The process \((X_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\) satisfies the Markov property if and only if for every \(n \in \mathbb{N}\) and every \(f \in \mathcal{B}_b(S, \mathcal{B})\), \[
\mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_n] = \mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid X_n] \quad \text{a.s.}
\tag{78.3}\]


Proof. The forward implication (\(\Longrightarrow\)) is immediate by setting \(s = n\) and \(t = n+1\) in Equation 78.1.
For the reverse implication (\(\Longleftarrow\)), we proceed by induction on \(n \ge k\) for a fixed base time \(k \in \mathbb{N}\).

Base Case (\(n = k+1\)): By assumption Equation 78.3, \(\mathbb{E}[f(X_{k+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] = \mathbb{E}[f(X_{k+1}) \mid X_k]\) a.s.
Induction Hypothesis: Assume that for some \(n &gt; k\), for all \(g \in \mathcal{B}_b(S, \mathcal{B})\), \[
\mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] = \mathbb{E}[g(X_n) \mid X_k] \quad \text{a.s.}\]
Inductive Step: Consider \(f(X_{n+1})\). By Equation 78.3 and Proposition 78.1, there exists \(q_{n+1} \in \mathcal{B}_b(S, \mathcal{B})\) such that: \[
\mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_n] = q_{n+1}(X_n) \quad \text{a.s.}
\] Using the tower property of conditional expectation since \(\mathcal{F}_k \subseteq \mathcal{F}_n\): \[
\begin{aligned}   
\mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] &= \mathbb{E}\left[ \mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_n] \mid \mathcal{F}_k \right] \\   &= \mathbb{E}[q_{n+1}(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_k].   
\end{aligned}
\] Applying the induction hypothesis to \(g = q_{n+1}\), we get: \[
\mathbb{E}[q_{n+1}(X_n) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] = \mathbb{E}[q_{n+1}(X_n) \mid X_k] \quad \text{a.s.}
\] Recombining these equalities gives: \[
\begin{aligned}   
\mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] &= \mathbb{E}[q_{n+1}(X_n) \mid X_k] \\   &= \mathbb{E}\left[ \mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_n] \mid X_k \right] \\   &= \mathbb{E}[f(X_{n+1}) \mid X_k] \quad \text{a.s.},   
\end{aligned}
\] which completes the induction step.

18.1 Definitions and Basic Properties
Let \((\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)\) be a measure space. We denote the space of non-negative \(\mathcal{F}/\mathcal{B}([0, \infty])\)-measurable functions by: \[
\begin{align*}
L^+ &= L^+(\Omega, \mathcal{F}) \\
&:= \{ f: \Omega \to [0, \infty] \mid f \text{ is } \mathcal{F}/\mathcal{B}([0, \infty])\text{-measurable} \}
\end{align*}
\tag{18.1}\]

Definition 25.1 The space \(L^2(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)\) is the collection of all real-valued, \(\mathcal{F}\)-measurable functions \(f: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}\) that are square-integrable: \[
\begin{aligned}
&L^2(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)\\
&\triangleq \left\{ f: \Omega \to \mathbb{R} \;\middle|\; f \text{ is } \mathcal{F}\text{-measurable and } \int_{\Omega} f^2 \, d\mu &lt; \infty. \right\}
\end{aligned}
\]

26.5 Total Number of Heads
Define the total number of heads \(S_n \triangleq \sum_{j=1}^n X_j\). By linearity of expectation: \[
\mathbb{E}[S_n] = \sum_{j=1}^n \mathbb{E}[X_j] = \frac{n}{2}
\]
The range of \(S_n(\omega)\) is \(\{0, 1, 2, \dots, n\}\). The probability mass function is: \[
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}(S_n = k) &= \frac{1}{2^n} \# \{ \omega \in \Omega \mid \omega_1 + \dots + \omega_n = k \} \\
&= \frac{1}{2^n} \binom{n}{k}, \quad k \in \{0, 1, \dots, n\}
\end{align*}
\]
Thus, \(S_n \sim \text{Bin}\left(n, \frac{1}{2}\right)\).

Definition 28.1 For \(1 \le p &lt; \infty\), the space \(L^p(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)\) is defined as the set of equivalence classes of \(\mathcal{F}\)-measurable functions \(f: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}\) under \(\mu\)-almost sure equality such that \(|f|^p\) is integrable: \[
L^p(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu) \triangleq \left\{ f \in L^0(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu) \;\middle|\; \int_{\Omega} |f|^p \, d\mu &lt; \infty \right\} \Bigg/ \sim_{\mu\text{-a.s.}}
\tag{28.1}\]


Theorem 28.1 (Minkowski’s Inequality and \(L^p\) Norm) For \(1 \le p &lt; \infty\), the function \(\|\cdot\|_{L^p}: L^p(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu) \to [0, \infty)\) defined by: \[
\|f\|_{L^p} \triangleq \left( \int_{\Omega} |f|^p \, d\mu \right)^{1/p}
\tag{28.2}\]
defines a norm on \(L^p(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)\). In particular, Minkowski’s inequality holds for all \(f, g \in L^p\): \[
\|f + g\|_{L^p} \le \|f\|_{L^p} + \|g\|_{L^p}
\tag{28.3}\]
Thus, \(L^p(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)\) is a normed vector space.

Definition 29.1 A set \(\mathbb{H}\) of \(\mathbb{R}\)-valued functions on \(\Omega\) is closed under bounded convergence if for every sequence \(\{f_n\} \subset \mathbb{H}\) satisfying:

Uniform Boundedness: \(\exists M &lt; \infty\) such that \(|f_n(\omega)| \le M\) for all \(n \in \mathbb{N}\) and all \(\omega \in \Omega\);
Pointwise Convergence: \(\lim_{n \to \infty} f_n(\omega) = f(\omega) \in \mathbb{R}\) for all \(\omega \in \Omega\);

the limit function \(f\) belongs to \(\mathbb{H}\) (\(f \in \mathbb{H}\)).


Notation: \(\mathbb{B}(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{R}) \triangleq \{ f: \Omega \to \mathbb{R} \mid f \text{ is } \mathcal{F}\text{-measurable and bounded} \}\). When \(\mathcal{F} = 2^\Omega\), we write \(\mathbb{B}(\Omega) \triangleq \mathbb{B}(\Omega, 2^\Omega, \mathbb{R})\).
Non-Examples: The spaces \(C_c(\mathbb{R})\) (compactly supported continuous functions) and \(C_b(\mathbb{R})\) (bounded continuous functions) are not closed under bounded convergence.

31.3 Fubini’s Theorem for Integrable Functions

Theorem 31.2 (Fubini’s Theorem) Let \(f: \Omega_1 \times \Omega_2 \to \mathbb{R}\) be \((\mathcal{F}_1 \otimes \mathcal{F}_2)\)-measurable. The following statements are equivalent:

\(f \in L^1(\Omega_1 \times \Omega_2, \mathcal{F}_1 \otimes \mathcal{F}_2, \mu_1 \otimes \mu_2)\);
\(\int_{\Omega_1} \left( \int_{\Omega_2} |f(\omega_1, \omega_2)| \, \mu_2(d\omega_2) \right) \mu_1(d\omega_1) &lt; \infty\);
\(\int_{\Omega_2} \left( \int_{\Omega_1} |f(\omega_1, \omega_2)| \, \mu_1(d\omega_1) \right) \mu_2(d\omega_2) &lt; \infty\).

If any (and hence all) of these conditions hold, then:

The slice function \(\omega_2 \mapsto f(\omega_1, \omega_2)\) is in \(L^1(\mu_2)\) for \(\mu_1\)-almost every \(\omega_1 \in \Omega_1\);
The slice function \(\omega_1 \mapsto f(\omega_1, \omega_2)\) is in \(L^1(\mu_1)\) for \(\mu_2\)-almost every \(\omega_2 \in \Omega_2\);
The integrated slice function \(\omega_1 \mapsto \int_{\Omega_2} f(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_2(d\omega_2)\) (defined to be \(0\) where the slice integral is undefined or infinite) belongs to \(L^1(\Omega_1, \mathcal{F}_1, \mu_1)\);
The integrated slice function \(\omega_2 \mapsto \int_{\Omega_1} f(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_1(d\omega_1)\) belongs to \(L^1(\Omega_2, \mathcal{F}_2, \mu_2)\);
The integral with respect to the product measure equals both iterated integrals: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\Omega_1 \times \Omega_2} f \, d(\mu_1 \otimes \mu_2) &= \int_{\Omega_1} \left( \int_{\Omega_2} f(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_2(d\omega_2) \right) \mu_1(d\omega_1) \\
&= \int_{\Omega_2} \left( \int_{\Omega_1} f(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_1(d\omega_1) \right) \mu_2(d\omega_2)
\end{aligned}
\tag{31.5}\]



Proof (Proof of Theorem 31.2). Step 1: Equivalence of Integrability Conditions. By Tonelli’s theorem applied to the non-negative function \(|f|\), the double integral \(\int |f| \, d(\mu_1 \otimes \mu_2)\) equals both iterated integrals of \(|f|\). Hence (1), (2), and (3) are equivalent.
Step 2: Null Set Analysis. Let \(E_1 \triangleq \left\{ \omega_1 \in \Omega_1 \;\middle|\; \int_{\Omega_2} |f(\omega_1, \omega_2)| \, \mu_2(d\omega_2) = \infty \right\}\). By Tonelli’s theorem: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\Omega_1} \left( \int_{\Omega_2} |f(\omega_1, \omega_2)| \, \mu_2(d\omega_2) \right) \mu_1(d\omega_1) = \int_{\Omega_1 \times \Omega_2} |f| \, d(\mu_1 \otimes \mu_2) &lt; \infty
\end{aligned}
\] Since the non-negative integrand \(\omega_1 \mapsto \int_{\Omega_2} |f(\omega_1,\omega_2)|\,\mu_2(d\omega_2)\) has a finite \(\mu_1\)-integral, it is \(\mu_1\)-a.e. finite. Indeed, for any \(n \ge 1\), Markov’s inequality gives \[
\mu_1(E_1) \le \mu_1\!\left(\int_{\Omega_2}|f(\omega_1,\cdot)|\,d\mu_2 \ge n\right) \le \frac{1}{n}\int_{\Omega_1 \times \Omega_2}|f|\,d(\mu_1\otimes\mu_2),
\] and since the right side tends to \(0\) as \(n \to \infty\), we conclude \(\mu_1(E_1) = 0\).
Step 3: Decomposition into Positive and Negative Parts. Decompose \(f = f^+ - f^-\) where \(f^+, f^- \ge 0\). For \(\omega_1 \in E_1^c\), both \(\int f^+(\omega_1, \cdot) \, d\mu_2 &lt; \infty\) and \(\int f^-(\omega_1, \cdot) \, d\mu_2 &lt; \infty\). Defining \(\int_{\Omega_2} f(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_2(d\omega_2) = 0\) on \(E_1\) gives: \[
\begin{aligned}
&\int_{\Omega_2} f(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_2(d\omega_2)\\
&= \mathbf{1}_{E_1^c}(\omega_1) \left( \int_{\Omega_2} f^+(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_2(d\omega_2) - \int_{\Omega_2} f^-(\omega_1, \omega_2) \, \mu_2(d\omega_2) \right)
\end{aligned}
\]
By Tonelli’s theorem applied to \(f^+\) and \(f^-\) (both non-negative), the functions \(\omega_1 \mapsto \int_{\Omega_2} f^\pm(\omega_1,\omega_2)\,\mu_2(d\omega_2)\) are \(\mathcal{F}_1\)-measurable, and each has finite \(\mu_1\)-integral since \(f^\pm \le |f|\) and \(f \in L^1(\mu_1 \otimes \mu_2)\). Integrating over \(\Omega_1\) and using \(\mu_1(E_1) = 0\) to drop the \(\mathbf{1}_{E_1^c}\) indicator: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\Omega_1} \left( \int_{\Omega_2} f \, d\mu_2 \right) d\mu_1
&= \int_{\Omega_1} \mathbf{1}_{E_1^c}(\omega_1)\left(\int_{\Omega_2} f^+ \, d\mu_2 - \int_{\Omega_2} f^- \, d\mu_2\right) d\mu_1 \\
&= \int_{\Omega_1}\int_{\Omega_2} f^+ \, d\mu_2 \, d\mu_1 - \int_{\Omega_1}\int_{\Omega_2} f^- \, d\mu_2 \, d\mu_1 \\
&= \int_{\Omega_1 \times \Omega_2} f^+ \, d(\mu_1 \otimes \mu_2) - \int_{\Omega_1 \times \Omega_2} f^- \, d(\mu_1 \otimes \mu_2) \\
&= \int_{\Omega_1 \times \Omega_2} f \, d(\mu_1 \otimes \mu_2)
\end{aligned}
\] The second line uses linearity of the outer integral (both terms are finite), the third applies Tonelli to \(f^+\) and \(f^-\) separately, and the last uses linearity of the product integral. Repeating the same argument with \(\Omega_1\) and \(\Omega_2\) swapped completes the proof.

38.4 Extension to Infinite Mean Cases
We close this section by discussing the extension of the Strong Law of Large Numbers to cases where the random variables have infinite mean.

Corollary 38.2 Let \(\{X_n\}_{n=1}^\infty\) be i.i.d. random variables with \(X_n \notin L^1\) but \(X_n^- \in L^1\) (so \(E[X_n] = +\infty\)). Then \(\frac{S_n}{n} \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}} +\infty\).


Proof (Proof of Corollary 38.2). For any threshold \(M &lt; \infty\), define \(X_n^M \triangleq X_n \wedge M = \min(X_n, M) \in L^1\). Applying Kolmogorov’s SLLN to the sequence \(\{X_n^M\}_{n=1}^\infty\) yields \(\frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=1}^n X_k^M \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}} E[X_1 \wedge M]\). Since \(X_n \ge X_n^M\), we have: \[
\begin{aligned}
\liminf_{n \to \infty} \frac{S_n}{n} &\ge \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=1}^n X_k^M \\
&= E[X_1 \wedge M] \\
&= E[(X_1 \wedge M)^+] - E[(X_1 \wedge M)^-] \quad \text{a.s.}
\end{aligned}
\tag{38.8}\] Taking \(M \to \infty\), Monotone Convergence Theorem implies \(E[X_1 \wedge M] \uparrow E[X_1] = +\infty\), proving \(\frac{S_n}{n} \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}} +\infty\).

40.2 An Alternating Sequence
Consider \(x_n=\frac{(-1)^n}{\sqrt{n}}\) and \(b_n=n\). Then \(\frac{x_n}{b_n} = \frac{(-1)^n}{n^{3/2}}\). The corresponding series converges absolutely because \[
\sum_{n=1}^\infty
\left|
\frac{x_n}{b_n}
\right|
=
\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{1}{n^{3/2}}
&lt;\infty.
\] Therefore, Kronecker’s Lemma implies \[
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=1}^n
\frac{(-1)^k}{\sqrt{k}}
\longrightarrow0.
\] Equivalently, \[
\boxed{
\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{(-1)^k}{\sqrt{k}}
=
o(n).
}
\] This example illustrates an important feature of Kronecker’s Lemma. The lemma does not say that the partial sums \(\sum_{k=1}^n x_k\) must converge. Rather, it says that their growth is negligible relative to \(b_n\): \(\sum_{k=1}^n x_k=o(b_n)\). Here, \(b_n=n\), so the conclusion is that the partial sums grow more slowly than linearly.
